1
00:00:04,070 --> 00:00:07,491
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I've got the Honey Bowl Sutta, Majjhima 18.

2
00:00:08,011 --> 00:00:11,872
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I guess, like, three key bits.

3
00:00:12,172 --> 00:00:15,413
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'll just maybe read out those main bits.

4
00:00:15,613 --> 00:00:18,073
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, let's start with the first one.

5
00:00:18,573 --> 00:00:29,496
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, the Buddha's gone somewhere and encounters a layman, Sakyan Dandapani.

6
00:00:30,336 --> 00:00:32,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Dandapani asks the Buddha,

7
00:00:36,585 --> 00:00:38,506
[SPEAKER_02]: The ascetic is one who says what?

8
00:00:38,746 --> 00:00:39,587
[SPEAKER_02]: One who declares what?

9
00:00:42,368 --> 00:00:43,309
[SPEAKER_02]: And the Buddha responds.

10
00:00:43,669 --> 00:01:03,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Friend, one who says in such a way that one stands having disputed with nobody in the world, with its gods, Maras and Brahmins, with its ascetics and Brahmins, with this generation of gods and humans, and also in such a way that dwelling detached from sense desires,

11
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:15,429
[SPEAKER_02]: That brahman is free from doubt, has cut remorse, without craving for this or that existence, perceptions are not underlying.

12
00:01:15,629 --> 00:01:16,810
[SPEAKER_01]: It's talking about an arahant.

13
00:01:17,791 --> 00:01:19,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how do you stop disputing with Mara?

14
00:01:21,334 --> 00:01:23,116
[SPEAKER_01]: By taking up what belongs to Mara.

15
00:01:24,237 --> 00:01:32,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Means by completely removing any appropriation and ownership in regard to your own eye, and sights, nose, and smells, and so on.

16
00:01:33,075 --> 00:01:38,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the mind completely developed, removed of any traces of greed, aversion, delusion.

17
00:01:38,916 --> 00:01:47,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you... it's inconceivable to have a dispute internally, let alone externally, with other people and so on.

18
00:01:47,158 --> 00:01:50,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't actually refer to just all dispute.

19
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:54,900
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more than that.

20
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:55,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

21
00:01:55,040 --> 00:02:02,362
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sort of, eh... any conflict.

22
00:02:02,904 --> 00:02:05,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, any discrepancy, yeah, you're free from.

23
00:02:05,726 --> 00:02:16,111
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, dispute is a discrepancy, the common-sense dispute, but the way they use it, it's quite clear that it applies even to on the level of that internal sense-basis that belong to Māra.

24
00:02:17,232 --> 00:02:22,515
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're disputing with Māra by claiming the ownership of all over those things, and that's a discrepancy.

25
00:02:27,597 --> 00:02:32,380
[SPEAKER_02]: So the Buddha finishes and says, friend, I'm one who says this, I'm one who declares this.

26
00:02:36,166 --> 00:02:36,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the ultimate goal.

27
00:02:39,708 --> 00:02:44,292
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the Buddha then leaves.

28
00:02:44,372 --> 00:02:46,994
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have a question about that particular bit?

29
00:02:47,114 --> 00:02:48,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, no, much further.

30
00:02:48,736 --> 00:02:49,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

31
00:02:49,536 --> 00:02:58,103
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then the Buddha goes away and speaks to the bhikkhus and tells them about his encounter with Dandapani.

32
00:03:01,383 --> 00:03:17,598
[SPEAKER_02]: And, in response to that, one of the bhikkhus says, but Bhante, the Buddha is one who says, what, such that he stands having disputed with nobody in the world, whether it's gods, Maras, Brahmins, Siddhics and Brahmins, with this generation of gods and humans.

33
00:03:18,696 --> 00:03:32,303
[SPEAKER_02]: And also, how is it that the Buddha, dwelling detached from sense desires, is that Brahman who is free from doubt, has cut remorse, without craving for this or that existence, and perceptions are not underlying?

34
00:03:33,964 --> 00:03:43,889
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the Bhikkhu responds... sorry, the Buddha responds, says, Bhikkhu, for what reason are proliferated perceptions and concepts in play for a man?

35
00:03:46,978 --> 00:03:55,522
[SPEAKER_02]: If there isn't anything to delight in, to welcome, to attach to here, just this is the end of the underlying tendency to passion.

36
00:03:57,262 --> 00:04:12,549
[SPEAKER_02]: Just this is the end of the underlying tendency to repulsion, the underlying tendency to fuse, the underlying tendency to doubt, conceit, the underlying tendency to... for being, and the underlying tendency to ignorance.

37
00:04:13,871 --> 00:04:19,856
[SPEAKER_02]: Just this is the end of taking up sticks, taking up swords, disputes, quarrels, arguments, criticisms, slander, false speech.

38
00:04:20,597 --> 00:04:23,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Here, these evil unwholesome things cease without remainder.

39
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:25,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

40
00:04:25,241 --> 00:04:27,462
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about the removal of the conflict.

41
00:04:27,943 --> 00:04:29,985
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about the removal of the basis of conflict.

42
00:04:30,525 --> 00:04:33,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessary basis for any type of conflict, internally or externally.

43
00:04:34,969 --> 00:04:39,192
[SPEAKER_01]: And the other thing I would point out, the pañca, sañña, saṅkha, the

44
00:04:40,297 --> 00:04:43,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Proliferation or diversification...

45
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:47,462
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of perception, as it clearly says there.

46
00:04:48,302 --> 00:04:49,723
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the result of your perception.

47
00:04:51,044 --> 00:04:57,968
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, all you need to do is just stop engaging with the results of your perception and thinking you're somehow undoing papāñcā.

48
00:04:59,029 --> 00:05:01,891
[SPEAKER_01]: So, papāñcā is on the level of thing being perceived.

49
00:05:02,571 --> 00:05:06,574
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you undo papāñcā, you undo the being, you undo the craving for being and everything else.

50
00:05:08,002 --> 00:05:18,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And by that, I mean, the meaning, significance, emotional value in regard to things that are being perceived, that's what papāñcay is.

51
00:05:18,708 --> 00:05:22,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Dependence, your dependence on ownership, is what papāñcay is.

52
00:05:23,810 --> 00:05:29,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, if I show you, I don't know, a watch, a random watch.

53
00:05:29,953 --> 00:05:32,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's a watch, but you don't feel anything towards it.

54
00:05:33,963 --> 00:05:39,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your perception, the meaning of that perception, the significance of it is, oh, this is not my watch and I don't need the watch.

55
00:05:40,109 --> 00:05:51,219
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, I show you a watch given to you by your granddad that he sort of survived two world wars and, you know, made you promise you're going to look after it and you were emotional.

56
00:05:51,299 --> 00:05:53,041
[SPEAKER_01]: It has so much significance to you.

57
00:05:53,922 --> 00:05:54,662
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the watch.

58
00:05:54,822 --> 00:05:55,443
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a watch.

59
00:05:55,643 --> 00:05:58,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Same, same perception of the same thing with same usefulness.

60
00:05:59,404 --> 00:06:01,085
[SPEAKER_01]: But it means much, much more.

61
00:06:01,405 --> 00:06:01,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

62
00:06:01,705 --> 00:06:12,830
[SPEAKER_01]: It has much more proliferated meaning, which means it has much more proliferated pressure on you, much more proliferated burden on you, much more proliferated being over you.

63
00:06:14,611 --> 00:06:16,152
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what papāñca is.

64
00:06:16,212 --> 00:06:23,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, taking that even further, for an ordinary person... Well, see, if somebody destroys the watch you don't care about, would you be upset with them?

65
00:06:24,284 --> 00:06:24,404
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

66
00:06:24,544 --> 00:06:25,184
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the thing.

67
00:06:25,204 --> 00:06:30,987
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody comes and destroys the watch that you're emotionally engaged with, you will take up the rod and try to beat them up.

68
00:06:31,547 --> 00:06:43,272
[SPEAKER_02]: But what I mean is, even so, for an ordinary person, even if they see a watch that isn't mine, they need to find other things that are theirs in order to undo their papāñca.

69
00:06:44,261 --> 00:06:47,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking about the watch is nothing to do with papāñcā.

70
00:06:47,702 --> 00:06:54,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking about things that mean a lot to you, yeah, that's already rooted in papāñcā and will increase the significance.

71
00:06:54,343 --> 00:06:55,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you forget about your granddad.

72
00:06:55,984 --> 00:06:58,844
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop thinking about him.

73
00:06:59,124 --> 00:07:01,645
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be experiencing daily sadnesses and so on.

74
00:07:02,005 --> 00:07:03,785
[SPEAKER_01]: But imagine you revolve around those things.

75
00:07:05,566 --> 00:07:09,786
[SPEAKER_01]: So that watch will be a constant reminder of all the emotional significance you're going through.

76
00:07:10,326 --> 00:07:11,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's going to grow.

77
00:07:11,207 --> 00:07:12,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Papāñcā will grow and increase.

78
00:07:13,792 --> 00:07:19,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I just gave the example of a random object in the world that can be meaningful or meaningless, but there are certain things that are always meaningful.

79
00:07:21,077 --> 00:07:26,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Your life, your body, your being, your senses, your eyes, your nose, and that's already proliferated.

80
00:07:27,022 --> 00:07:27,242
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

81
00:07:27,662 --> 00:07:38,330
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you wonder whether it is or not, well, would you be concerned, would you suffer, would you experience mental anguish if you go blind, if you become disabled?

82
00:07:39,031 --> 00:07:41,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be emotionally disrupted on account of it?

83
00:07:41,353 --> 00:07:41,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you would.

84
00:07:42,967 --> 00:07:48,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to that extent, the meaning of these things have proliferated into your own being, so to speak.

85
00:07:48,713 --> 00:07:49,654
[SPEAKER_01]: You are it.

86
00:07:50,055 --> 00:07:51,656
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're responsible for that proliferation.

87
00:07:51,676 --> 00:07:53,158
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not in things.

88
00:07:53,198 --> 00:07:54,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception is in things, so to speak.

89
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:58,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Perceiving the watch, perceiving things with your eyes, that's it.

90
00:07:58,523 --> 00:08:00,645
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, in the scene, there is just the scene.

91
00:08:00,665 --> 00:08:03,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And for an arahant in the scene, there will be just the scene.

92
00:08:03,752 --> 00:08:12,503
[SPEAKER_01]: All the meaning, the pressure, the emotional connection, the ownership, the appropriation has been fully depleted, destroyed, removed for an arahant.

93
00:08:13,203 --> 00:08:14,265
[SPEAKER_02]: Things are still meaningful.

94
00:08:15,038 --> 00:08:19,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, things are still meaningful on the level of its own perception.

95
00:08:19,279 --> 00:08:26,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would still have a memory that this watch used to be very meaningful, very important to me, because it was given to me by a granddad.

96
00:08:26,121 --> 00:08:31,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the significance of things that's being perceived does not disappear.

97
00:08:31,383 --> 00:08:33,683
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like for an arahant, he doesn't know what watch is for.

98
00:08:34,043 --> 00:08:34,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

99
00:08:34,683 --> 00:08:39,965
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that that pressuring burden of the emotional significance has been now uprooted.

100
00:08:40,825 --> 00:08:43,666
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, this is still an important and extremely useful watch.

101
00:08:46,220 --> 00:09:00,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when we... proliferation, the papāñcā side of things, it's not in things that you perceive, nor even their significance and their use and their intentionality.

102
00:09:00,290 --> 00:09:01,551
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, with that, that all remains.

103
00:09:01,751 --> 00:09:02,732
[SPEAKER_01]: That remains completely.

104
00:09:03,212 --> 00:09:07,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And what... and that's what Guru said, if you remove the papāñcā, what's left is like a...

105
00:09:08,444 --> 00:09:09,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Tree cut off at its root.

106
00:09:10,285 --> 00:09:16,789
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove the life of this growth, but what has been grown stays, petrified, like branches of a tree.

107
00:09:17,270 --> 00:09:18,410
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what people don't realize.

108
00:09:18,751 --> 00:09:23,094
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they immediately, like, jump in a conclusion, like, oh, I must throw away my possessions.

109
00:09:23,554 --> 00:09:24,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look, you have that phone.

110
00:09:24,775 --> 00:09:26,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you must be attached to it.

111
00:09:27,076 --> 00:09:30,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Attachment does not mean possession, so to speak.

112
00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:38,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Things... the meaning of this is that it's mine, but the meaning of mine does not have to have an emotional influence over me.

113
00:09:39,507 --> 00:09:44,671
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's much harder to discern, because it's much easier to just throw away the phone, seemingly.

114
00:09:45,072 --> 00:09:48,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the more... self-mortification comes as a result of that attitude.

115
00:09:48,855 --> 00:09:57,241
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll throw away my possession, I'll throw away my wealth, I'll throw away my clothes, I will throw away my eyes, I will destroy this body and that's how I will reach enlightenment.

116
00:09:57,281 --> 00:09:57,441
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

117
00:09:57,541 --> 00:10:03,506
[SPEAKER_01]: That's utterly, utterly misguided on the equal footing as going towards the sense object like sensuality is.

118
00:10:06,048 --> 00:10:07,109
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the middle way.

119
00:10:10,011 --> 00:10:14,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, if somebody else randomly, as that man, did what you're teaching, that's exactly what the Buddha would describe.

120
00:10:14,914 --> 00:10:15,715
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the teaching.

121
00:10:25,759 --> 00:10:39,852
[SPEAKER_02]: Could you say, then, that the particular... the removal of papāñcā is basically the removal of a whole sort of domain of meaning that's about... Well, let's put it in simpler terms.

122
00:10:40,493 --> 00:10:50,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Removal of papāñcā is on the basis of things that have an emotional weight over you.

123
00:10:52,373 --> 00:11:02,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, the removal of atta, all these things, and that's again, it's, that's what we keep mentioning in other talks, when, you know, there's the common views are, oh, look, everything is impermanent.

124
00:11:02,557 --> 00:11:03,858
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's, that's unimportant.

125
00:11:04,118 --> 00:11:04,758
[SPEAKER_01]: That's irrelevant.

126
00:11:04,999 --> 00:11:05,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether everything is...

127
00:11:06,299 --> 00:11:10,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that are there to you, are the ones you need to contemplate it for being impermanent.

128
00:11:10,401 --> 00:11:12,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that will make you feel suffering if you lose them.

129
00:11:13,893 --> 00:11:15,795
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, that's why people don't want to think about it.

130
00:11:16,116 --> 00:11:22,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Even without losing things, just thinking about a possibility of losing things that are dear to you, is already suffering.

131
00:11:23,244 --> 00:11:24,546
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what pañca is.

132
00:11:26,228 --> 00:11:29,451
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that dearness of it increases the more you adhere to it.

133
00:11:30,377 --> 00:11:33,600
[SPEAKER_01]: The less you question it, the more you refuse to look at it, it grows.

134
00:11:34,101 --> 00:11:38,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the necessary basis for pañca growing is ignoring pañca.

135
00:11:39,506 --> 00:11:43,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring the weight of this emotional significance it has over me.

136
00:11:43,950 --> 00:11:45,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Turning away from it because it's unpleasant.

137
00:11:46,712 --> 00:11:48,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring your responsibility for it.

138
00:11:48,594 --> 00:11:48,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

139
00:11:48,814 --> 00:11:50,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Because responsibility is unpleasant.

140
00:11:53,026 --> 00:12:03,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to undo papāñca, you need to find things that are dearest to you, and start cultivating perception of impermanence, while those things are felt as dear to me.

141
00:12:04,613 --> 00:12:04,853
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

142
00:12:06,234 --> 00:12:14,299
[SPEAKER_01]: But before that, you need to practice sense restraint, because otherwise you... you might go mad, or overwhelmed with anxiety and fear.

143
00:12:15,149 --> 00:12:18,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Fall on the ground, beat your breast and pull your hair, as the suttas describe.

144
00:12:19,816 --> 00:12:24,543
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, if you're going to be contemplating the nature of the body, let's say,

145
00:12:25,851 --> 00:12:30,535
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it's quite easy to forget your... how... how dear it is to you.

146
00:12:30,755 --> 00:12:31,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

147
00:12:31,536 --> 00:12:32,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

148
00:12:32,317 --> 00:12:32,917
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I always...

149
00:12:32,937 --> 00:12:35,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Some abstract notion of a, you know, objective body.

150
00:12:35,319 --> 00:12:40,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I always criticize this, like, meditation techniques and body sensations and watching, because it's impersonal.

151
00:12:40,423 --> 00:12:40,964
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

152
00:12:41,624 --> 00:12:47,850
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the necessary basis for practice, which is recognized the personal involvement, is completely taken away.

153
00:12:47,870 --> 00:12:48,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

154
00:12:48,610 --> 00:12:50,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Repressed, removed, non-acknowledged.

155
00:12:51,487 --> 00:12:51,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And why?

156
00:12:51,987 --> 00:12:53,128
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's unpleasant.

157
00:12:53,829 --> 00:12:56,791
[SPEAKER_01]: No, don't don't look at your knees and your shoulders and sensations.

158
00:12:57,211 --> 00:12:59,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Look at the value that the body has for you.

159
00:13:00,493 --> 00:13:02,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I suffer if I die next five minutes?

160
00:13:02,895 --> 00:13:06,718
[SPEAKER_01]: If I find out I might die next five minutes, will I experience anguish and fear?

161
00:13:07,398 --> 00:13:09,840
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of sensation will reveal that to you.

162
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:14,723
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all secondary to your first and foremost framework of your relationship with that body.

163
00:13:16,825 --> 00:13:18,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to do a meditation technique,

164
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:26,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Find the ways that your own body is dear to you, and then question that, by contemplating impermanence in regard to it.

165
00:13:28,964 --> 00:13:34,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by denying, oh, look, it's all impermanent, it's all impermanent, thus my idea of this is dear to me is a mistaken idea.

166
00:13:34,826 --> 00:13:38,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, from an arahant's point of view, it's a mistaken idea, because he had undone it.

167
00:13:39,728 --> 00:13:42,429
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't undone it, so it's a factual idea.

168
00:13:42,909 --> 00:13:44,890
[SPEAKER_01]: The mistake is real as a mistake.

169
00:13:45,690 --> 00:13:47,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And unless you start acknowledging it first,

170
00:13:48,856 --> 00:13:50,577
[SPEAKER_01]: All you do is denying it.

171
00:13:53,019 --> 00:13:54,240
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you'll never resolve it.

172
00:14:07,829 --> 00:14:15,274
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so... after the Buddha says that, he... he goes off to his dwelling.

173
00:14:16,598 --> 00:14:19,819
[SPEAKER_02]: It's one of those suttas where the bhikkhus are like, what did you mean by that?

174
00:14:19,839 --> 00:14:20,619
[SPEAKER_02]: And they didn't ask him.

175
00:14:20,979 --> 00:14:22,159
[SPEAKER_02]: And they didn't ask him.

176
00:14:22,179 --> 00:14:23,620
[SPEAKER_02]: And they say, who should we ask?

177
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:28,221
[SPEAKER_02]: And they are, Mahakacana, he's good at doing this thing.

178
00:14:28,361 --> 00:14:31,862
[SPEAKER_02]: He's always explaining in detail what the Buddha said in brief.

179
00:14:32,402 --> 00:14:33,222
