[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

376
00:24:55,069 --> 00:24:55,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And the...

377
00:24:58,174 --> 00:25:02,736
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

378
00:25:03,016 --> 00:25:06,758
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about undoing that or trying to re-root.

379
00:25:07,778 --> 00:25:10,359
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about uprooting the necessary basis.

380
00:25:10,879 --> 00:25:11,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, everything else is left.

381
00:25:11,960 --> 00:25:12,920
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

382
00:25:13,560 --> 00:25:20,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even the preferences, to some extent, preferences based upon sensuality, for example, you uproot the sensuality, the

383
00:25:22,377 --> 00:25:26,879
[SPEAKER_01]: The dried-up, petrified directions of your preferences are still there.

384
00:25:26,999 --> 00:25:31,301
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why arahants could remember things of an unwholesome nature in the past.

385
00:25:31,781 --> 00:25:39,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, I used to act out of fear, and I was hiding in my palace, because I was fearing for my life, and who knows what else he was doing on account of that fear.

386
00:25:39,525 --> 00:25:49,149
[SPEAKER_01]: He remembers that, but he feels none of it, because he has removed the basis of that fear, the dependence on how he felt, trying to protect, shelter himself from the unpleasant feelings.

387
00:25:50,442 --> 00:25:53,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean that he literally became like a whiteboard.

388
00:25:53,845 --> 00:25:56,286
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no memory, no recollection, no trace of anything.

389
00:25:56,867 --> 00:25:57,287
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

390
00:25:57,687 --> 00:25:59,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I used to really delight in this.

391
00:25:59,909 --> 00:26:02,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, obviously, I find it disgusting.

392
00:26:02,331 --> 00:26:11,417
[SPEAKER_01]: But he remembers that the extent of delight, that the extent of the perception that grew as a result of him engaging even more with the meaning of it that was pressuring him.

393
00:26:13,439 --> 00:26:16,001
[SPEAKER_01]: So, removal of pañca does not remove the perception.

394
00:26:16,801 --> 00:26:18,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Removal of pañca does not remove your thoughts.

395
00:26:20,607 --> 00:26:22,089
[SPEAKER_02]: So, actually, yeah, that's that's the...

396
00:26:22,129 --> 00:26:26,273
[SPEAKER_02]: So, with the support of the contact, or the support of the pressure, there's feeling.

397
00:26:27,194 --> 00:26:30,237
[SPEAKER_02]: What one feels, one perceives.

398
00:26:30,257 --> 00:26:31,959
[SPEAKER_02]: What one perceives, one thinks about.

399
00:26:32,740 --> 00:26:34,301
[SPEAKER_02]: What one thinks about, one proliferates.

400
00:26:35,623 --> 00:26:37,325
[SPEAKER_02]: What one proliferates.

401
00:26:37,585 --> 00:26:38,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that...

402
00:26:38,165 --> 00:26:39,987
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all, see, within the pressure.

403
00:26:40,388 --> 00:26:40,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

404
00:26:41,670 --> 00:26:45,751
[SPEAKER_01]: The basis of the pressure remains holding all of these further factors that the Buddha describes.

405
00:26:45,771 --> 00:26:48,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that they cease once the next factor comes.

406
00:26:49,032 --> 00:26:50,513
[SPEAKER_01]: They're all within the pressure.

407
00:26:51,033 --> 00:26:57,355
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the pressure, there is no more growth of the pressure through your thinking and perceiving and revolving around it.

408
00:26:59,973 --> 00:27:01,894
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but if there's no pressure, there's no feeling.

409
00:27:01,914 --> 00:27:06,636
[SPEAKER_02]: If there's no feeling, there's no perceiving, there's no perceiving, there's no thinking about, there's no thinking about, there's no proliferation.

410
00:27:07,676 --> 00:27:18,721
[SPEAKER_02]: What one proliferates, because of that proliferated perceptions and concepts, are in play for a man, in terms of sights that he can be conscious of, with the eye in the past, in the future, in the present.

411
00:27:18,741 --> 00:27:20,462
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the same for ears and sounds.

412
00:27:22,723 --> 00:27:24,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think you could, you could sum that up by saying

413
00:27:26,868 --> 00:27:29,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about thinking about what you perceive that it's a problem.

414
00:27:31,372 --> 00:27:37,377
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about thinking about what you perceive on account of being pressured by it, that's the problem.

415
00:27:37,918 --> 00:27:40,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thinking out of the pressure is the problem.

416
00:27:41,341 --> 00:27:41,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

417
00:27:42,081 --> 00:27:46,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Not about what you think and what movements your mind makes.

418
00:27:46,685 --> 00:27:48,507
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the fact that you're giving in to the pressure.

419
00:27:49,599 --> 00:27:52,481
[SPEAKER_01]: And you want to get rid of the pressure through thoughts and figuring it out.

420
00:27:52,901 --> 00:27:55,202
[SPEAKER_02]: Or thinking while that pressure is there, can you say?

421
00:27:55,282 --> 00:27:58,184
[SPEAKER_02]: But like, but that pressure is only there because you don't understand what the pressure is.

422
00:27:58,224 --> 00:28:01,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, you have the thoughts as a result of the pressure, but that's one thing.

423
00:28:01,746 --> 00:28:05,668
[SPEAKER_01]: And, but, you know, as the Buddha said, you don't delight in the thought of sensuality.

424
00:28:05,728 --> 00:28:06,729
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't entertain it.

425
00:28:06,769 --> 00:28:07,670
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't engage with it.

426
00:28:07,690 --> 00:28:08,310
[SPEAKER_01]: But then that would be...

427
00:28:08,330 --> 00:28:10,031
[SPEAKER_02]: Thought of sensuality is already pressure.

428
00:28:10,991 --> 00:28:11,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

429
00:28:11,632 --> 00:28:12,152
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

430
00:28:12,192 --> 00:28:12,392
[SPEAKER_02]: If you...

431
00:28:12,432 --> 00:28:14,574
[SPEAKER_01]: If you keep delighting in it... Yeah.

432
00:28:14,854 --> 00:28:14,934
[SPEAKER_01]: You

433
00:28:17,780 --> 00:28:19,142
[SPEAKER_02]: You're making it worse, so to speak.

434
00:28:19,862 --> 00:28:21,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Not... if you don't have the thought at all.

435
00:28:22,105 --> 00:28:23,767
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... you can't choose that.

436
00:28:24,207 --> 00:28:26,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Thought is there, as a result of the pressure, sure.

437
00:28:27,491 --> 00:28:29,753
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize, that's not up to me.

438
00:28:30,334 --> 00:28:32,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Up to me is whether I keep feeding the pressure.

439
00:28:32,576 --> 00:28:35,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you stop feeding the pressure, the pressure will dissolve.

440
00:28:36,677 --> 00:28:44,621
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't just choose to cancel the pressure by choosing to cancel the thought, because that's another thought rooted in the same pressure.

441
00:28:45,321 --> 00:28:47,462
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when people say, oh, too much thinking results in pain.

442
00:28:47,522 --> 00:28:47,682
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

443
00:28:48,163 --> 00:28:56,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Too much trying to escape the pressure results in more pressure, because your wanting to escape pressure is rooted by... in that pressure.

444
00:28:57,047 --> 00:28:58,588
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't do so if you're not pressured.

445
00:28:58,828 --> 00:28:59,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

446
00:29:00,249 --> 00:29:01,029
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's actually...

447
00:29:01,368 --> 00:29:06,691
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to acknowledge the pressure and then learn how not to be pressured while pressure is there.

448
00:29:07,472 --> 00:29:09,413
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where sensory strain needs to come first.

449
00:29:09,953 --> 00:29:13,275
[SPEAKER_01]: That already enables you to endure the discomfort to some extent.

450
00:29:16,937 --> 00:29:18,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So, too much thinking is not the problem.

451
00:29:18,978 --> 00:29:22,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning how to... Too much acting out of pressure is the problem.

452
00:29:22,060 --> 00:29:22,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

453
00:29:23,041 --> 00:29:27,223
[SPEAKER_02]: Learning how to not be pressured by the pressure... Is the way out of pressure.

454
00:29:27,464 --> 00:29:29,805
[SPEAKER_01]: If, yeah, if you... if you do that,

455
00:29:33,606 --> 00:29:34,326
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no pressure.

456
00:29:34,466 --> 00:29:34,646
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

457
00:29:35,687 --> 00:29:36,447
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be pressured.

458
00:29:36,527 --> 00:29:37,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is no pressure anymore.

459
00:29:38,387 --> 00:29:40,048
[SPEAKER_02]: If I'm not pressured by the pressure...

460
00:29:40,108 --> 00:29:42,449
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you start saying... That's not pressure anymore.

461
00:29:42,529 --> 00:29:45,890
[SPEAKER_02]: If you start saying, there's no pressure... Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, okay.

462
00:29:46,490 --> 00:29:48,331
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, stop thinking, stop thinking.

463
00:29:48,611 --> 00:29:49,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop the pressure, stop the pressure.

464
00:29:50,691 --> 00:29:51,772
[SPEAKER_01]: You do that out of the pressure.

465
00:29:52,352 --> 00:29:54,492
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you act out of the pressure, means you feed the pressure.

466
00:29:54,753 --> 00:29:56,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it has to be knowing that you're free.

467
00:29:57,313 --> 00:29:59,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first you have to acknowledge the pressure.

468
00:29:59,054 --> 00:29:59,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

469
00:29:59,534 --> 00:30:02,395
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to acknowledge the thinking and realize that that's not the problem.

470
00:30:03,860 --> 00:30:05,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensual perceptions are not a problem.

471
00:30:05,741 --> 00:30:06,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of those is the problem.

472
00:30:07,982 --> 00:30:11,084
[SPEAKER_01]: But you act out of those because you can't endure those perceptions for what they are.

473
00:30:11,284 --> 00:30:12,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you give in to the pressure.

474
00:30:13,845 --> 00:30:18,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And by giving in to the pressure, you're pressured by definition of it.

475
00:30:19,628 --> 00:30:23,110
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to acknowledge it and allow it to be, allow it to endure.

476
00:30:24,611 --> 00:30:29,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you then learn how not to be bothered by its endurance, by its presence.

477
00:30:30,174 --> 00:30:31,595
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how it will wither away.

478
00:30:32,898 --> 00:30:34,859
[SPEAKER_02]: You, you stop engaging with it.

479
00:30:34,879 --> 00:30:35,780
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop feeding it.

480
00:30:38,882 --> 00:30:39,963
[SPEAKER_02]: Allow it to do what it does.

481
00:30:45,826 --> 00:30:49,129
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it goes through all the senses, the same, that same sort of formula.

482
00:30:51,850 --> 00:30:53,231
[SPEAKER_02]: And then there's just a slight bit.

483
00:31:05,368 --> 00:31:16,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Friends, when there is an eye, when there are sights, when there is eye consciousness, that the manifestation of pressure will be discerned, that is possible.

484
00:31:19,195 --> 00:31:24,878
[SPEAKER_02]: When there is the manifestation of pressure, that the manifestation of feeling will be discerned, that is possible.

485
00:31:25,678 --> 00:31:30,120
[SPEAKER_02]: When there is the manifestation of feeling, that the manifestation of perception will be discerned, that is possible.

486
00:31:31,661 --> 00:31:33,342
[SPEAKER_01]: When... You can also say the same to craving.

487
00:31:34,592 --> 00:31:35,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

488
00:31:35,092 --> 00:31:38,193
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no manifestation of feeling, there is nothing to crave for or against.

489
00:31:39,874 --> 00:31:41,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, okay.

490
00:31:42,075 --> 00:31:46,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove the pressure, you remove pressure being felt.

491
00:31:47,316 --> 00:31:52,278
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the pressure, you remove that pressure being felt, being craved against or for it.

492
00:31:52,778 --> 00:31:54,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove the pressure, you remove the suffering.

493
00:31:55,319 --> 00:31:59,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by removing the craving in regard to pressure, there is no suffering anymore.

494
00:31:59,901 --> 00:32:00,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what suffering is.

495
00:32:01,995 --> 00:32:04,497
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in the eye, the object's not in the consciousness.

496
00:32:06,058 --> 00:32:08,319
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in you revolving around the pressure that you're under.

497
00:32:08,819 --> 00:32:21,607
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that's why this suit is sort of unusual, or like... because it brings in the notion of perception to the kind of normal paticca-saṅgha.

498
00:32:21,627 --> 00:32:23,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I think it's just another way of describing it.

499
00:32:23,048 --> 00:32:30,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is the other paticca-saṅgha part of it, that after craving goes into taking up the roads and fighting and killing each other, and leaves all the other factors out.

500
00:32:30,713 --> 00:32:31,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

501
00:32:33,628 --> 00:32:40,250
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh... when there is a manifestation of perception, that the manifestation of thinking will be discerned, that is possible.

502
00:32:40,690 --> 00:32:52,235
[SPEAKER_02]: When there is the manifestation of thinking, that the manifestation of proliferated perceptions and concepts, eh... being at play, or being in play, will be discerned, that is possible.

503
00:32:52,255 --> 00:32:54,075
[SPEAKER_02]: And the same for the other senses.

504
00:33:00,197 --> 00:33:01,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... the point there is, again,

505
00:33:02,313 --> 00:33:04,634
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that the thing or things' perceptions grow.

506
00:33:05,975 --> 00:33:08,896
[SPEAKER_01]: The proliferation is the meaning of those perceptions.

507
00:33:08,936 --> 00:33:11,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, I... again, back onto the original example.

508
00:33:11,217 --> 00:33:12,218
[SPEAKER_01]: I show you a watch.

509
00:33:14,559 --> 00:33:16,900
[SPEAKER_01]: It means nothing to you and you don't have any need for it.

510
00:33:16,980 --> 00:33:18,721
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you get a need for a watch.

511
00:33:19,561 --> 00:33:23,823
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the perception of the same watch, nothing... it didn't change the color or increase its size.

512
00:33:23,843 --> 00:33:24,043
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

513
00:33:24,083 --> 00:33:27,065
[SPEAKER_01]: The perception is identical, but it means more.

514
00:33:27,185 --> 00:33:30,867
[SPEAKER_01]: It has more weight now, because there is a certain need that can be met by that.

515
00:33:31,692 --> 00:33:36,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, imagine you then really, really become dependent on knowing the time at every given moment.

516
00:33:37,649 --> 00:33:43,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're really dependent on having a watch and now that watch is even more meaningful to you.

517
00:33:43,993 --> 00:33:46,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you find out maybe it's also a very expensive watch.

518
00:33:46,874 --> 00:33:50,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you also find out it was owned by the King of England or something like that.

519
00:33:50,236 --> 00:33:53,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's extra valuable to all other people and it's yours.

520
00:33:53,738 --> 00:33:56,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's the same perception of the same watch.

521
00:33:56,460 --> 00:33:59,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing has changed, yet everything has changed.

522
00:33:59,982 --> 00:34:03,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also when people have difficulties understanding an arahant.

523
00:34:04,882 --> 00:34:07,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking like, you know, he stops perceiving and he just flies in the air.

524
00:34:07,803 --> 00:34:07,963
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

525
00:34:08,464 --> 00:34:10,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, externally, nothing has changed.

526
00:34:12,385 --> 00:34:13,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally, everything has changed.

527
00:34:13,966 --> 00:34:16,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize that's what everything is.

528
00:34:16,727 --> 00:34:20,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's pressure or non-pressure determines everything.

529
00:34:21,690 --> 00:34:23,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're putujana or an arahant.

530
00:34:23,891 --> 00:34:27,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're with the grain of the pressure or against the grain of the pressure.

531
00:34:27,672 --> 00:34:28,673
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, not pressured.

532
00:34:34,734 --> 00:34:36,656
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, there he goes on to describe the arahants.

533
00:34:38,137 --> 00:34:46,623
[SPEAKER_02]: So, friends, when there isn't an eye, when there aren't sights, when there isn't high consciousness, that the manifestation of pressure will be discerned, that is impossible.

534
00:34:47,023 --> 00:34:51,407
[SPEAKER_01]: And you might wonder, so why didn't the Buddha then just stay and stick with the pressure?

535
00:34:52,227 --> 00:34:56,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's your starting point for practice, it's the first point of contact.

536
00:34:56,931 --> 00:35:01,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did he tell you what the pressure is dependent upon?

537
00:35:01,074 --> 00:35:01,835
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think that is?

538
00:35:03,911 --> 00:35:07,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did you put a point in the necessary basis for the pressure that is your first point?

539
00:35:07,534 --> 00:35:08,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is your first point.

540
00:35:09,015 --> 00:35:10,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Why did you point out the necessary basis for it?

541
00:35:11,117 --> 00:35:16,541
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can only undermine that necessary basis for everything, the pressure.

542
00:35:17,021 --> 00:35:18,282
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only undermine the pressure?

543
00:35:18,563 --> 00:35:20,464
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that that is also... that's determined.

544
00:35:20,684 --> 00:35:21,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

545
00:35:21,185 --> 00:35:24,467
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that it doesn't depend on you, your choice, your attention.

546
00:35:24,988 --> 00:35:27,310
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that it's subjected to something that's not in your control.

547
00:35:28,070 --> 00:35:32,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you being pressured is equally subjected to that basis that's not in your control.

548
00:35:32,152 --> 00:35:34,193
[SPEAKER_02]: So, everything, everything that you...

549
00:35:34,213 --> 00:35:44,339
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why if you think... See, that's why the only intelligible relationship with I, objects and consciousness, is to be seen as a necessary basis for that which is experienced.

550
00:35:44,359 --> 00:35:48,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't experience I, objects and consciousness separately.

551
00:35:48,881 --> 00:35:52,223
[SPEAKER_01]: You only get to discern it through the first point, which is the pressure.

552
00:35:52,363 --> 00:35:53,904
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no lower point than the pressure.

553
00:35:53,984 --> 00:35:54,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

554
00:35:56,997 --> 00:35:59,458
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the saṅkhara, in its definition.

555
00:36:01,518 --> 00:36:10,121
[SPEAKER_02]: Anguttarinikaya Sutta says, like, you know, the one that all things converge on feeling, and what's the thing about contact there?

556
00:36:11,282 --> 00:36:11,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, yes.

557
00:36:12,802 --> 00:36:17,564
[SPEAKER_01]: They're directed by contact, or something like that.

558
00:36:17,624 --> 00:36:18,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Governed by mindfulness.

559
00:36:19,604 --> 00:36:21,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Converge upon feeling.

560
00:36:22,480 --> 00:36:24,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Revolve around the contact, or something like that.

561
00:36:24,802 --> 00:36:25,102
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

562
00:36:25,462 --> 00:36:27,824
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not speaking about transactions, but yeah, yeah, something like that.

563
00:36:27,844 --> 00:36:31,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, yeah, everything has to all come... No, that's what I mean.

564
00:36:31,886 --> 00:36:35,708
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot go below the contact, because that means you won't be contacted.

565
00:36:36,009 --> 00:36:36,209
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

566
00:36:36,329 --> 00:36:38,570
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're going below, it means that is what contacts you.

567
00:36:39,010 --> 00:36:44,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're always within the framework, within the, literally, the container of the contact, of the pressure.

568
00:36:45,201 --> 00:36:47,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And I prefer pressure, because contact is impersonal.

569
00:36:47,902 --> 00:36:48,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's contact.

570
00:36:49,243 --> 00:36:50,583
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's already pressure, isn't it?

571
00:36:51,264 --> 00:36:51,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

572
00:36:52,524 --> 00:36:58,967
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to find, like, emotional... Not, like, exactly, like, emotionally disinterested contacts, like, because you just happen to engage with it.

573
00:36:59,467 --> 00:37:01,628
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's the emotional contact, which means emotional pressure.

574
00:37:01,788 --> 00:37:03,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

575
00:37:03,789 --> 00:37:06,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha described the necessary basis for pressure.

576
00:37:07,362 --> 00:37:13,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the only way you can really understand and get the right perspective on that pressure and thus stop being pressured by it.

577
00:37:14,623 --> 00:37:20,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you need to walk out of the pressure into the necessary basis for it and somehow free yourself from the pressure.

578
00:37:20,865 --> 00:37:21,585
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

579
00:37:23,345 --> 00:37:31,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the saṅkharas, for the necessary pressure that you experience, for the first point, the bottom, is the pressure.

580
00:37:31,907 --> 00:37:35,968
[SPEAKER_01]: The saṅkharas are basically the right context how you should regard the same pressure.

581
00:37:36,648 --> 00:37:36,888
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

582
00:37:39,367 --> 00:37:44,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding the pressure is only out by understanding what is determined by.

583
00:37:45,391 --> 00:37:47,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, at the moment, you see pressure.

584
00:37:47,093 --> 00:37:47,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is mine.

585
00:37:48,293 --> 00:37:50,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, acting out of pressure, it's also mine.

586
00:37:50,695 --> 00:37:52,677
[SPEAKER_01]: It's also for the purpose of what I want to do, and so on.

587
00:37:53,937 --> 00:38:00,042
[SPEAKER_01]: But, by seeing pressure as actually not being in your control, not being rooted in, already you're learning its correct nature.

588
00:38:00,802 --> 00:38:03,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And, by doing so, already you're less affected by that pressure.

589
00:38:04,125 --> 00:38:07,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And, is it conceivable to act out of pressure, if you're not pressured?

590
00:38:08,419 --> 00:38:08,599
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

591
00:38:09,160 --> 00:38:12,342
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you act towards those things... You're acting, but that's not the same thing.

592
00:38:12,362 --> 00:38:14,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Would an act be rooted in the pressure if you don't feel the pressure?

593
00:38:15,144 --> 00:38:15,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

594
00:38:15,385 --> 00:38:15,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

595
00:38:16,405 --> 00:38:18,307
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's another way... And that's how arahant can still act.

596
00:38:18,407 --> 00:38:20,369
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why there'd be no feeling, there would be no... Yeah.

597
00:38:20,589 --> 00:38:20,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

598
00:38:21,069 --> 00:38:22,010
[SPEAKER_01]: No pressured feeling.

599
00:38:22,190 --> 00:38:22,390
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

600
00:38:22,771 --> 00:38:23,771
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

601
00:38:23,791 --> 00:38:25,893
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how arahant can still choose and prefer.

602
00:38:26,193 --> 00:38:26,834
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't like this.

603
00:38:27,154 --> 00:38:28,835
[SPEAKER_01]: So in... Tell this person to go away.

604
00:38:29,496 --> 00:38:30,697
[SPEAKER_01]: He's disagreeable.

605
00:38:30,917 --> 00:38:31,137
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

606
00:38:31,337 --> 00:38:32,018
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a fool.

607
00:38:32,078 --> 00:38:33,259
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to spend my time with him.

608
00:38:33,719 --> 00:38:33,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh

609
00:38:37,579 --> 00:38:38,319
[SPEAKER_01]: He can discriminate.

610
00:38:38,439 --> 00:38:41,860
[SPEAKER_01]: None of his discrimination will be rooted in being pressured by the discrimination.

611
00:38:43,480 --> 00:38:43,740
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

612
00:38:43,760 --> 00:38:56,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in one sense, you can say... And if you actually make the effort and read the origin stories for the... as in, not you, but the audience, whoever is interested in this, about the rules, you'll see how discriminative the Buddha was.

613
00:38:56,483 --> 00:38:57,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

614
00:38:58,003 --> 00:38:59,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the language he used.

615
00:38:59,944 --> 00:39:00,424
[SPEAKER_01]: You would think...

616
00:39:00,444 --> 00:39:01,464
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember first time I read it.

617
00:39:01,544 --> 00:39:05,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, you get this... as a layman, this is the arahant, basically, just this sort of...

618
00:39:06,792 --> 00:39:09,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Energy of everything and nothing.

619
00:39:09,734 --> 00:39:14,676
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you start reading this, like, when the Buddha starts, like, blasting the monks who, you know, misbehave.

620
00:39:15,536 --> 00:39:16,977
[SPEAKER_01]: In the foolish mouth.

621
00:39:17,217 --> 00:39:19,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Better you should better use the snake's mouth and whatnot.

622
00:39:20,099 --> 00:39:20,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

623
00:39:20,879 --> 00:39:23,841
[SPEAKER_01]: You think, how can an arahant speak like that?

624
00:39:24,761 --> 00:39:27,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't fathom anything in between.

625
00:39:28,723 --> 00:39:31,925
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, no, you can't, because it's a factual right thing to say.

626
00:39:34,623 --> 00:39:36,125
[SPEAKER_01]: And none of that is rooted in the pressure.

627
00:39:36,145 --> 00:39:41,732
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha is not emotionally disturbed on account of monks doing what he says or monks not doing what he says.

628
00:39:42,393 --> 00:39:45,657
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's still, oh, this is disagreeable, this is stupid, this is foolish behavior.

629
00:39:46,218 --> 00:39:47,880
[SPEAKER_01]: This is agreeable, this is a good behavior.

630
00:39:48,641 --> 00:39:51,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, either way, I'm not affected by it, because he's not pressured by it.

631
00:39:54,319 --> 00:40:04,792
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also those cases of arahants and the suttas, where people, well, complained about it, because the way they were speaking was rude and seemingly conceited and arrogant, and they were upsetting everybody left and right.

632
00:40:05,353 --> 00:40:10,079
[SPEAKER_01]: They complained to the Buddha and said, no, it's just literally that the habits of the speech have left.

633
00:40:10,798 --> 00:40:14,481
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that is rooted in pressure of vanity, pressure of craving, pressure of conceit.

634
00:40:14,501 --> 00:40:24,087
[SPEAKER_01]: He used to work in a fishyard, and he used to spend his time... Or the other one was the... always been born in the higher Brahmin family, and he's just used to talking down to people.

635
00:40:24,528 --> 00:40:26,489
[SPEAKER_01]: He's, like, literally the habit of his speech.

636
00:40:27,870 --> 00:40:38,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, if you judge things simply based on that, nothing, like, there's nothing else that you can discern within it, yeah, you're missing out, completely.

637
00:40:40,128 --> 00:40:42,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not seeing where the problem is.

638
00:40:42,369 --> 00:40:46,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when you will be blaming the eye, the sights, for your pressure, for your papāñcā.

639
00:40:47,390 --> 00:40:49,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you think all I need to do is to stop seeing.

640
00:40:49,990 --> 00:40:52,131
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how I will approve the papāñcā of my sights.

641
00:40:52,351 --> 00:40:52,611
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

642
00:40:52,891 --> 00:40:54,151
[SPEAKER_01]: The significance of my sights.

643
00:40:55,812 --> 00:41:00,753
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll get rid of that eye that's gone on to do this pressure I'm feeling right now.

644
00:41:00,773 --> 00:41:00,913
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

645
00:41:01,897 --> 00:41:05,340
[SPEAKER_01]: By getting rid of the basis of this pressure, I will get rid of the pressure.

646
00:41:05,380 --> 00:41:08,282
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to know the basis of the pressure is through the pressure.

647
00:41:09,042 --> 00:41:13,826
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in practice, all you're doing is acting out of pressure, which is what perpetuates the pressure.

648
00:41:14,406 --> 00:41:16,287
[SPEAKER_01]: The basis doesn't perpetuate the pressure.

649
00:41:16,387 --> 00:41:18,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance of the basis perpetuates the pressure.

650
00:41:18,609 --> 00:41:22,192
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you know the basis if you destroy the basis?

651
00:41:22,252 --> 00:41:24,914
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you understand the eye if you lose your eyes and sights?

652
00:41:25,074 --> 00:41:27,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, better practice until it's too late.

653
00:41:29,037 --> 00:41:29,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise,

654
00:41:30,085 --> 00:41:35,909
[SPEAKER_01]: If pressure were truly rooted in those things, as a pressure that pressures you, death would be an arahantship for everyone.

655
00:41:37,450 --> 00:41:39,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Destruction of the senses would be a nibbāna.

656
00:41:39,372 --> 00:41:40,252
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not the case.

657
00:41:49,248 --> 00:41:53,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the term for proliferation, you could use pressure as well?

658
00:41:54,091 --> 00:41:54,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

659
00:41:54,452 --> 00:41:54,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure...

660
00:41:54,992 --> 00:41:57,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Proliferation is a result of acting out of pressure.

661
00:41:57,874 --> 00:42:00,415
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you pressure by finding something that will meet your need.

662
00:42:01,616 --> 00:42:08,741
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, your needs of, as I gave the example, of needing the watch to see the time, is already rooted in how you feel about it.

663
00:42:09,001 --> 00:42:14,365
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're already, to some degree, acting towards comfort, towards pleasant feeling.

664
00:42:14,925 --> 00:42:18,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need... Oh, this item can meet that purpose, so that item becomes more significant.

665
00:42:21,131 --> 00:42:24,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And the next time you perceive that item, you already perceive it with that significance.

666
00:42:24,854 --> 00:42:25,875
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to remind yourself.

667
00:42:25,895 --> 00:42:26,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait.

668
00:42:26,535 --> 00:42:27,396
[SPEAKER_01]: What is this for?

669
00:42:28,116 --> 00:42:28,256
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

670
00:42:28,357 --> 00:42:40,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Next time you see the watch, especially the purpose, emotional purpose, emotional need is still there, any watch you see will be seen with more significance, because that's what you need it for.

671
00:42:40,286 --> 00:42:42,448
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been increasing the value of this watch.

672
00:42:42,468 --> 00:42:42,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

673
00:42:42,849 --> 00:42:44,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Within the same perception of the same thing.

674
00:42:45,211 --> 00:42:45,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

675
00:42:47,372 --> 00:42:48,053
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

676
00:42:49,414 --> 00:43:04,299
[SPEAKER_00]: The point, when attending to the contact, it's like people, like you said, people take what's impersonal and they attend to these impersonal phenomena and they're like, oh, look at me, I'm becoming free from it.

677
00:43:04,319 --> 00:43:04,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

678
00:43:05,120 --> 00:43:08,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you just pick the impersonal ones and think that they will overwhelm

679
00:43:09,201 --> 00:43:10,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever personal you're taking.

680
00:43:10,742 --> 00:43:13,622
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to be looking where the conceit is, the appropriation.

681
00:43:13,642 --> 00:43:24,506
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to find the personal ones, and then you realize that any any correct form of practicing impersonal perceptions can only be done so within the framework of personal.

682
00:43:25,886 --> 00:43:28,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about replacing personal with impersonal.

683
00:43:29,247 --> 00:43:32,188
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about seeing impersonal through the personal.

684
00:43:33,114 --> 00:43:39,660
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of sabbe-saṅkhara-anicca, sabbe-saṅkhara-dukkha, sabbe-dhamma-anatta.

685
00:43:42,123 --> 00:43:47,107
[SPEAKER_01]: But for most people it's sabbe-dhamma-anicca, sabbe-dhamma-dukkha, sabbe-dhamma-anatta.

686
00:43:47,287 --> 00:43:50,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is impermanent, everything is unpleasant, everything is not self.

687
00:43:50,791 --> 00:43:51,011
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

688
00:43:53,153 --> 00:43:54,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessary basis is impermanent.

689
00:43:54,714 --> 00:43:56,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessary basis is unpleasant.

690
00:43:56,936 --> 00:43:58,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Your ownership is not your own.

691
00:44:01,110 --> 00:44:11,373
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you ignore the basis of ownership as a starting point, you'll be looking at these random things and just trying to psychologize the anicca and re-explain it to you.

692
00:44:11,713 --> 00:44:12,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the other way around.

693
00:44:12,634 --> 00:44:16,055
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to reverse the picture by taking up what's first for you.

694
00:44:16,155 --> 00:44:22,297
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's first for you is me, my sense of ownership, what pressures me, emotional need, emotional dependence.

695
00:44:22,737 --> 00:44:24,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, within that emotional need...

696
00:44:24,637 --> 00:44:26,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, take a person that's dear to you.

697
00:44:27,752 --> 00:44:38,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Without ignoring the fact that they're dear to you, without stopping to, like, ceasing to feel the dearness, so to speak, think about their impermanence and see how you're going to feel about it.

698
00:44:40,845 --> 00:44:48,372
[SPEAKER_00]: It's possible to attend to that proliferation but not be beset by it, like if you have... What do you mean by attending to it?

699
00:44:48,633 --> 00:44:48,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Like...

700
00:44:49,719 --> 00:44:51,762
[SPEAKER_00]: You know proliferation is occurring.

701
00:44:52,123 --> 00:44:52,924
[SPEAKER_00]: That you can...

702
00:44:52,944 --> 00:44:54,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Proliferation is not occurring.

703
00:44:55,148 --> 00:44:56,109
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already occurred.

704
00:44:56,209 --> 00:45:00,616
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already the basis upon which you can either choose to act or restrain for.

705
00:45:01,758 --> 00:45:03,861
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not like, oh, proliferation is a process.

706
00:45:05,243 --> 00:45:05,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

707
00:45:06,844 --> 00:45:10,486
[SPEAKER_00]: From before, I guess, like, I would go to these places and speak about proliferation.

708
00:45:10,506 --> 00:45:10,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

709
00:45:10,826 --> 00:45:15,529
[SPEAKER_01]: People, as I said, they assume proliferation to be on the level of your mental activity.

710
00:45:15,569 --> 00:45:16,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, stop that.

711
00:45:16,350 --> 00:45:17,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop proliferating.

712
00:45:17,230 --> 00:45:17,330
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

713
00:45:17,430 --> 00:45:20,612
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of meaning and pressure of things that they exhibit over you.

714
00:45:21,333 --> 00:45:25,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, things that you have not emotionally engaged with, they have not proliferated for you.

715
00:45:25,935 --> 00:45:39,002
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, you start cultivating, entertaining, welcoming, acting out of a certain emotional engagement with the same thing, same perception of the same thing, that emotional dependence will grow.

716
00:45:39,882 --> 00:45:40,703
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the things that...

717
00:45:41,932 --> 00:45:50,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Things that move you, the things that you're emotionally moved by, they have already undergone... there is this proliferation in regard to that thing.

718
00:45:50,801 --> 00:45:52,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Because otherwise, nothing would matter to you.

719
00:45:52,623 --> 00:45:54,225
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's to do with the meaning of that thing.

720
00:45:54,265 --> 00:45:55,847
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not to do with anything I'm doing right now.

721
00:45:56,387 --> 00:45:57,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

722
00:45:57,569 --> 00:45:59,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So whatever you're doing right now will be rooted in that meaning.

723
00:46:01,151 --> 00:46:13,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you saying... you trying to stop your thinking because you think that that's how you stop a pañca, basically, is like saying, oh, so I'll stop thinking about the pressure because that's how I won't be pressured and the only reason I want to do this is because I'm pressured.

724
00:46:15,583 --> 00:46:19,106
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to stop thinking about the pressure because you're already pressured by the unpleasantness of it.

725
00:46:21,007 --> 00:46:22,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you stop thinking?

726
00:46:22,228 --> 00:46:23,209
[SPEAKER_01]: By thinking about it.

727
00:46:26,072 --> 00:46:27,192
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a contradiction in terms.

728
00:46:27,873 --> 00:46:28,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you not think?

729
00:46:28,794 --> 00:46:30,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't have any thoughts in your mind now.

730
00:46:31,635 --> 00:46:32,636
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a thought in your mind.

731
00:46:33,056 --> 00:46:34,557
[SPEAKER_01]: I must not have any thoughts in my mind.

732
00:46:35,558 --> 00:46:37,619
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's utterly futile, like completely.

733
00:46:38,420 --> 00:46:41,882
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stop and think about it, no pun intended, it's impossible.

734
00:46:43,003 --> 00:46:43,604
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

735
00:46:44,224 --> 00:46:49,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you blaming the result of pressure for the pressure means ignoring where the pressure is.

736
00:46:50,589 --> 00:46:51,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm not pressured.

737
00:46:51,930 --> 00:46:53,911
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only when I think about the pressure, I'm pressured.

738
00:46:54,571 --> 00:46:57,053
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I stop thinking about the pressure, that will free me from pressure.

739
00:46:57,193 --> 00:47:00,216
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, I'll ignore the pressure to be free from pressure.

740
00:47:01,273 --> 00:47:02,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the definition of avijjā.

741
00:47:03,714 --> 00:47:06,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of discomfort, you refuse to address things.

742
00:47:07,796 --> 00:47:10,356
[SPEAKER_02]: Out of discomfort, you ignore them.

743
00:47:10,557 --> 00:47:12,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of discomfort, you turn a blind eye.

744
00:47:21,181 --> 00:47:21,861
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like...

745
00:47:23,687 --> 00:47:33,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, throughout my day, or even week, or, you know, throughout my experiences, it would be like, okay, there will be certain kinds of thoughts.

746
00:47:34,103 --> 00:47:50,887
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, either about food or hunger or, you know, a certain experience I would like to re-experience, but I don't... like, the time without... like, if I were to think about that right now, oh, but I don't feel any pressure in regard to that.

747
00:47:50,947 --> 00:47:55,048
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, when there's a certain discomfort that happens to me, then there's the pressure.

748
00:47:55,128 --> 00:47:57,969
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the difference that people will remain unaware

749
00:47:59,405 --> 00:48:04,748
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as they think that the pressure is in the perception, that the pañca is in the perception.

750
00:48:05,108 --> 00:48:05,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

751
00:48:05,668 --> 00:48:07,529
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in regard to it, but it's not perceived.

752
00:48:08,870 --> 00:48:10,271
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the meaning of what you perceive.

753
00:48:10,311 --> 00:48:19,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, you have a random thought about a food, and maybe because you're factually hungry, but there is no pressure to eat.

754
00:48:19,195 --> 00:48:22,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You just had the thought about a food, and there's no problem with that thought.

755
00:48:23,878 --> 00:48:25,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, on another occasion, you have a thought about a food

756
00:48:26,564 --> 00:48:28,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're just full of sensuality and sensual craving.

757
00:48:28,946 --> 00:48:31,247
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not even hungry, yet you're not pressured.

758
00:48:32,328 --> 00:48:37,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, hunger will not pressure you towards food as much as your craving will pressure you towards food.

759
00:48:37,892 --> 00:48:39,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Hunger and food is just out there.

760
00:48:41,695 --> 00:48:50,161
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will never differentiate that, eating for the sake of hunger and eating for the sake of sensuality, for as long as you blame the thoughts of food for sensuality, for proliferation.

761
00:48:51,286 --> 00:48:51,766
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

762
00:48:51,806 --> 00:48:54,348
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you need to accept the fact that the mind is thinking about this.

763
00:48:54,548 --> 00:49:02,572
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can see whether you're thinking about it out of compulsive pressure, or out of factual necessity, or like a spontaneous arising of those thoughts.

764
00:49:04,794 --> 00:49:11,958
[SPEAKER_01]: But if every thought means pressure, every thought means proliferation, every thought must be stopped, you'll never see that whole domain of middle way.

765
00:49:13,078 --> 00:49:16,660
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be blaming thoughts and trying to cancel them.

766
00:49:16,800 --> 00:49:17,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Or ignoring them.

767
00:49:18,160 --> 00:49:20,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

768
00:49:20,082 --> 00:49:21,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Or distracting yourself from it.

769
00:49:21,464 --> 00:49:23,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

770
00:49:23,387 --> 00:49:24,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there are thoughts of food.

771
00:49:24,528 --> 00:49:25,029
[SPEAKER_01]: I must stop it.

772
00:49:25,069 --> 00:49:25,249
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

773
00:49:26,070 --> 00:49:30,175
[SPEAKER_01]: I must see whether this is rooted, as we say, in wholesome or rooted in unwholesome.

774
00:49:31,457 --> 00:49:32,097
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

775
00:49:32,378 --> 00:49:35,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you know to be unwholesome, abandon it.

776
00:49:36,672 --> 00:49:44,557
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, whatever you know that it's rooted in unwholesome, that is rooted in pressure, don't cultivate it, don't entertain it, don't delight in it, as he said.

777
00:49:45,058 --> 00:49:46,138
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you abandon it.

778
00:49:46,198 --> 00:49:47,259
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you stop feeding it.

779
00:49:47,839 --> 00:49:52,963
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean, if you approved craving for food, doesn't mean you'll stop eating for the rest of your life.

780
00:49:53,663 --> 00:49:57,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean you'll be unaware of the preferential taste, so to speak.

781
00:49:57,566 --> 00:50:01,869
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of it will be rooted in you craving, being pressured towards that food.

782
00:50:03,229 --> 00:50:05,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And consequently being upset on account of not getting it.

783
00:50:06,433 --> 00:50:11,176
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

784
00:50:11,196 --> 00:50:21,243
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can discern whether you're acting under pressure or not, is to first see what acting is occurring, what thoughts are there.

785
00:50:21,283 --> 00:50:27,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes it's bad to think about food, but not because of the thoughts of food, but because it's rooted in the pressure.

786
00:50:28,067 --> 00:50:32,310
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes it's not bad to think about food, because it won't be rooted in the pressure.

787
00:50:33,265 --> 00:50:36,066
[SPEAKER_01]: But if all you can see is thoughts of food, you'll never see where they're rooted.

788
00:50:36,207 --> 00:50:37,307
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll never see where the pressure is.

789
00:50:37,607 --> 00:50:40,449
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you'll never see where the dhamma is, in the practice.

790
00:50:45,231 --> 00:50:51,074
[SPEAKER_02]: And, if you can't see in that direction, you'll never see that that pressure... Well, you'll never get the right view.

791
00:50:51,474 --> 00:50:53,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that pressure is dependent on something.

792
00:50:54,076 --> 00:50:54,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

793
00:50:54,576 --> 00:50:56,177
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have any control over that.

